"""Flashcard format (hint + the math + airplane translation + breakdown): CMO topics 01-04 and 08."""
B = {
101: ("Which two properties must the airfield have so the lowest point can actually be reached?",
  [r"<strong>Closed = the fence belongs to the airfield,</strong> so a plane heading for the boundary can really land on it.",
   r"<strong>Bounded = a finite size,</strong> so the search cannot run off to infinity.",
   r"<strong>Heine-Borel:</strong> in $\mathbb R^n$ these two together are exactly compactness, the property Weierstrass needs."]),
102: ("What does the altimeter read at a cliff edge, and why does that keep the lowest point reachable?",
  [r"<strong>$\liminf_{x\to x_0}f(x)\ge f(x_0)$:</strong> the value at the edge is at or below everything you approach it with, so the true value sits on the low side of any jump.",
   r"<strong>Sublevel sets $\{f\le\alpha\}$:</strong> the region at or below altitude $\alpha$; being closed means the boundary of that region is landable.",
   r"<strong>The two statements are equivalent:</strong> jumps downward are allowed, jumps upward (a hole in the floor) are not."]),
103: ("What two conditions guarantee the pilot a lowest point he can actually touch down on?",
  [r"<strong>$K$ compact:</strong> the airfield is fenced (closed) and finite (bounded).",
   r"<strong>$f$ continuous:</strong> no cliffs or holes in the terrain.",
   r"<strong>Conclusion:</strong> a lowest point $x^*$ exists inside $K$, so a search over the airfield cannot come up empty."]),
104: ("Why can the pilot stop worrying about searching to infinity when the walls rise forever?",
  [r"<strong>$f(x)\to+\infty$ as $\|x\|\to\infty$:</strong> far from the airport every direction is a mountain wall.",
   r"<strong>The sublevel set $\{f\le\alpha\}$ is therefore bounded and closed:</strong> a fenced, finite airfield.",
   r"<strong>Then Weierstrass applies inside it:</strong> the lowest point exists in all of $\mathbb R^n$."]),
105: ("What region is the epigraph, and how does it show whether the terrain is convex?",
  [r"<strong>$\operatorname{epi}(f)=\{(x,t):f(x)\le t\}$</strong> is all the sky on or above the terrain, with height $t$ as the extra coordinate.",
   r"<strong>Convex terrain $\iff$ convex sky:</strong> any straight flight path between two points in that sky stays in it.",
   r"<strong>Why it matters:</strong> it turns a statement about a function into a statement about a set, where separating hyperplanes work."]),
106: ("How do you prove a lowest point exists on all of the plane when the airfield has no fence?",
  [r"<strong>The bound $4|x_1x_2|\le2(x_1^2+x_2^2)$</strong> shows $f\ge x_1^4+x_2^4-2(x_1^2+x_2^2)$, which rises without limit: $f$ is coercive.",
   r"<strong>Coercive means the lowest point is inside some finite fence,</strong> so Weierstrass guarantees a global minimum.",
   r"<strong>Setting $\nabla f=0$</strong> gives the candidates $(0,0)$ (a saddle, $f=0$) and $\pm(1,1)$ with $f=-2$: the true lowest points."]),
201: ("Which three instrument readings let the pilot model the terrain near his position?",
  [r"<strong>$f(x)$ = the altimeter.</strong> Your altitude right now.",
   r"<strong>$\nabla f(x)^\top d$ = the slope term.</strong> The altitude change from moving $d$ at the current slope; the dot product keeps only the part of $d$ pointing along the slope.",
   r"<strong>$\tfrac12d^\top\nabla^2f\,d$ = the curvature term.</strong> The extra correction because the slope itself bends as you move; the $\tfrac12$ and the square come from integrating a changing slope."]),
202: ("What must the slope gauge read at the very bottom of a valley?",
  [r"<strong>$\nabla f(x^*)=0$:</strong> the radar sees no slope in any direction.",
   r"<strong>Why:</strong> if any component were nonzero, moving against it would lose altitude, so $x^*$ could not be a lowest point.",
   r"<strong>Warning:</strong> it is only necessary. A hilltop or a saddle also reads zero."]),
203: ("What must the curvature gauge say at a lowest point so no direction rolls the marble away?",
  [r"<strong>$\nabla f(x^*)=0$</strong> removes the slope term of the Taylor expansion.",
   r"<strong>$\nabla^2f(x^*)\succeq0$ means $d^\top\nabla^2f\,d\ge0$ for every $d$:</strong> the ground curves up or stays flat in every direction.",
   r"<strong>If one direction had negative curvature,</strong> the plane would roll down it, so $x^*$ would not be a minimum."]),
204: ("What upgrade turns the necessary conditions into a guarantee of a strict valley?",
  [r"<strong>$\nabla f(x^*)=0$ and $\nabla^2f(x^*)\succ0$:</strong> zero slope and strictly positive curvature in every direction.",
   r"<strong>Strict positivity gives a bowl floor</strong> $d^\top\nabla^2f\,d\ge c\|d\|^2$ with $c\gt0$, so leaving $x^*$ always raises the altitude.",
   r"<strong>It is sufficient but not necessary:</strong> $f=x^4$ has a strict minimum at 0 even though its curvature there is 0."]),
205: ("What do the signs of the Hessian eigenvalues say about the terrain shape at a critical point?",
  [r"<strong>Each eigenvalue is the curvature along its own eigen-direction.</strong> Positive curves up, negative curves down.",
   r"<strong>All positive = valley bottom; all negative = hilltop;</strong> at least one of each sign = saddle (low along one axis, high along another).",
   r"<strong>At a saddle there is always a way down,</strong> along an eigenvector with negative curvature."]),
206: ("Why is $(1,1)$ a strict minimum of Rosenbrock even though the canyon is so hard to follow?",
  [r"<strong>$\det=400\gt0$ and $\operatorname{tr}=1002\gt0$</strong> for a 2×2 Hessian means both eigenvalues are positive, so $\nabla^2f\succ0$.",
   r"<strong>The two eigenvalues are very different</strong> (about 1000 and 0.4), so the floor is nearly flat compared with the walls.",
   r"<strong>That huge ratio is the condition number:</strong> it is why gradient methods crawl along this canyon."]),
301: ("What does 'the string above the terrain' say about convexity?",
  [r"<strong>$\theta x+(1-\theta)y$</strong> is a point on the straight flight path between $x$ and $y$.",
   r"<strong>$\theta f(x)+(1-\theta)f(y)$</strong> is the height of the straight string stretched between the two altitudes.",
   r"<strong>The inequality says the terrain never rises above the string,</strong> which is convexity."]),
302: ("What does the glide plane touching at your position say about the rest of the terrain?",
  [r"<strong>$f(x)+\nabla f(x)^\top(y-x)$</strong> is the flat plane through your position with the radar's slope.",
   r"<strong>$f(y)\ge$ that plane:</strong> the true terrain is never below its tangent plane.",
   r"<strong>Consequence:</strong> if $\nabla f(x)=0$ the plane is level and the terrain is everywhere above it, so $x$ is a global minimum."]),
303: ("What does 'the seat is always pressed up' say about the Hessian?",
  [r"<strong>$\nabla^2f(x)\succeq0$:</strong> in every direction the second derivative $d^\top\nabla^2f\,d$ is not negative, so the ground never curves away from the seat.",
   r"<strong>It must hold at every point of the set,</strong> not only at the bottom.",
   r"<strong>It is the practical test for convexity</strong> of a smooth function."]),
304: ("What bowl is the terrain guaranteed to stay above?",
  [r"<strong>$\nabla^2f\succeq\mu I$:</strong> the curvature is at least $\mu$ in every direction, never flatter than a bowl of steepness $\mu$.",
   r"<strong>Equivalent floor:</strong> $f(y)\ge f(x)+\nabla f(x)^\top(y-x)+\tfrac\mu2\|y-x\|^2$, the tangent plane plus a quadratic bowl.",
   r"<strong>Consequence:</strong> there is a unique lowest point and leaving it always costs altitude."]),
305: ("What does the PL inequality say about slope and height above the floor?",
  [r"<strong>$\|\nabla f\|^2$ = how steep the radar reads;</strong> $f-f^*$ = how high you still are above the floor.",
   r"<strong>The inequality says steepness $\ge2\mu\times$ height:</strong> the higher you are, the steeper the ground, so there are no plateaus far above the floor.",
   r"<strong>Then each leg of size $1/L$ removes a fixed fraction $\mu/L$ of the height,</strong> giving linear convergence without needing convexity."]),
306: ("What separates convex, strictly convex and strongly convex terrain?",
  [r"<strong>Convex:</strong> $\nabla^2f\succeq0$; level ramps are allowed.",
   r"<strong>Strictly convex:</strong> no flat stretch, but the curvature may vanish at a point (for example $x^4$ at 0).",
   r"<strong>Strongly convex:</strong> $\nabla^2f\succeq\mu I$ with a fixed $\mu\gt0$, a true bowl everywhere; each condition implies the one before it."]),
401: ("What must be true of the heading, and what does the step size decide?",
  [r"<strong>$d_k$ = the nose.</strong> The condition $\nabla f(x_k)^\top d_k\lt0$ means the nose has a component pointing downhill.",
   r"<strong>$\alpha_k$ = the flight distance.</strong> Multiplying turns the heading into an actual displacement.",
   r"<strong>Together:</strong> a good direction with a badly chosen distance can still overshoot or crawl, which is what line search fixes."]),
402: ("How far should the pilot fly along a straight track on a quadratic terrain?",
  [r"<strong>Numerator $-g^\top d$ = the downward momentum:</strong> how steeply the heading dives down the slope.",
   r"<strong>Denominator $d^\top Qd$ = the brakes:</strong> how fast the terrain curves back up along $d$.",
   r"<strong>The condition $\nabla f(x_{k+1})^\top d_k=0$:</strong> at the bottom of that track the slope along it is zero, so the new radar reading is perpendicular to the old heading."]),
403: ("How much altitude must a leg earn to be accepted?",
  [r"<strong>$\alpha\nabla f(x_k)^\top d_k$</strong> is the drop you would get if the slope stayed constant over the whole leg.",
   r"<strong>$c_1$</strong> demands only a fraction of that promised drop, because the terrain curves back up.",
   r"<strong>A leg that earns less than that is refused:</strong> too much fuel for too little altitude."]),
404: ("What stops the pilot from stopping early on a slope that is still steep?",
  [r"<strong>$|\nabla f(x_k+\alpha d)^\top d|$ = the slope along the heading after the leg.</strong>",
   r"<strong>The inequality demands it be at most $c_2$ times the starting slope:</strong> the slope must have flattened enough, so tiny hops are refused.",
   r"<strong>$0\lt c_1\lt c_2\lt1$</strong> keeps Armijo (not too far) and curvature (not too short) compatible: together they are the Wolfe conditions."]),
405: ("What must the nose never do, to guarantee the radar eventually reads zero?",
  [r"<strong>$\cos\theta_k$ measures the alignment</strong> of the heading with straight downhill. Sideways ($\theta=90^\circ$) means $\cos\theta=0$.",
   r"<strong>The sum $\sum\cos^2\theta_k\|g_k\|^2\lt\infty$</strong> forces its terms to 0.",
   r"<strong>If $\cos\theta_k\ge\delta\gt0$ always,</strong> that leaves $\|g_k\|\to0$: the plane reaches flat ground."]),
406: ("What does the pilot do when his planned leg earns too little altitude?",
  [r"<strong>Start with a bold step</strong> $\alpha^{(0)}$ (often 1).",
   r"<strong>Multiply by $\rho\in(0,1)$ each time</strong> the Armijo test fails: 'halve it and look again'.",
   r"<strong>Stop at the first success.</strong> It always ends, because a short enough leg always earns the promised drop."]),
801: ("What does the flight log reveal about the curvature, without surveying the whole valley?",
  [r"<strong>$s_k$ = how far you flew; $y_k$ = how much the slope reading changed.</strong>",
   r"<strong>$B_{k+1}s_k=y_k$:</strong> the curvature estimate must turn the displacement you made into the slope change you saw, which is exactly what the true Hessian does along a straight path.",
   r"<strong>$H_{k+1}y_k=s_k$ is the inverse form:</strong> it maps the slope change back to the displacement."]),
802: ("What is the cheapest possible correction that still satisfies the secant equation?",
  [r"<strong>$y_k-B_ks_k$ = the error:</strong> what the old estimate failed to predict about the slope change.",
   r"<strong>$vv^\top/(v^\top s)$ with $v=y_k-B_ks_k$</strong> adds a correction in exactly that direction, a rank-one update.",
   r"<strong>The denominator $(y_k-B_ks_k)^\top s_k$</strong> rescales it so the secant equation holds; if it is near 0 the update blows up."]),
803: ("Which two corrections does DFP apply to the inverse curvature estimate?",
  [r"<strong>$+\dfrac{s_ks_k^\top}{s_k^\top y_k}$ adds the leg you just flew,</strong> so the new estimate reproduces $H_{k+1}y_k=s_k$.",
   r"<strong>$-\dfrac{H_ky_ky_k^\top H_k}{y_k^\top H_ky_k}$ removes the old estimate's opinion</strong> along $H_ky_k$, which it would otherwise double-count.",
   r"<strong>Both are rank one and symmetric,</strong> so the update stays symmetric."]),
804: ("Why is BFGS the preferred quasi-Newton update?",
  [r"<strong>$\rho_k=1/y_k^\top s_k$ is one over the curvature measured along the last leg.</strong>",
   r"<strong>$(I-\rho sy^\top)H(I-\rho ys^\top)$ filters the old estimate</strong> so it agrees with the new secant condition, and $+\rho ss^\top$ adds the new leg back.",
   r"<strong>If $H_k\succ0$ and $y_k^\top s_k\gt0$, then $H_{k+1}\succ0$:</strong> every nose it produces points downhill."]),
805: ("What must the slope reading do along a leg for the curvature log to stay trustworthy?",
  [r"<strong>$s_k^\top y_k\gt0$:</strong> along the leg you flew, the slope reading rose, which means the terrain curved upward.",
   r"<strong>This is equivalent to $H_{k+1}$ staying positive definite,</strong> so the nose stays a descent direction.",
   r"<strong>The Wolfe curvature condition guarantees it,</strong> so a Wolfe line search makes BFGS safe."]),
806: ("What does the pilot keep from the flight log, and what does he throw away?",
  [r"<strong>Only the last $m$ pairs $(s,y)$:</strong> memory $O(mn)$ instead of the full $O(n^2)$ curvature map.",
   r"<strong>$\gamma_k=\frac{s^\top y}{y^\top y}$ rescales the starting guess $H_0=\gamma_kI$</strong> so it already matches the latest curvature.",
   r"<strong>Old legs are forgotten:</strong> nearly the same guidance for a fraction of the memory."]),
}
FIX = {cid: {"hint": h, "breakdown": b} for cid, (h, b) in B.items()}
